AB  - P. palmivora is a major causal agent of black pod disease in cacao, and its management still relieson chemical and cultural practices with variable efficacy. Bacterial consortia provide a promisingbiological-control platform because they can integrate complementary mechanisms; however, theextent to which direct antagonism, disease suppression, and host molecular responses areconnected remains poorly resolved in cacao. Here, four beneficial bacteria—Paenibacillustaichungensis B19, Pseudomonas juntendi B06, Bacillus megaterium DB1, and Bacillus velezensisBB1—were evaluated for proteolysis, cellulolysis, siderophore production, and antagonism againstDiaporthe sp., Neofusicoccum sp., Lasiodiplodia theobromae, and P. palmivora. The strains wereassembled into two consortia and tested in Theobroma cacao L. seedlings challenged with P.palmivora. Disease severity was quantified using a diagrammatic scale and digital lesion-areaanalysis, whereas host defense responses were assessed by qPCR of PR1, PR3, PR4, and PR10. B.velezensis displayed the broadest antagonistic phenotype, inhibiting all tested pathogens by 56.1–63.5%. In planta, consortium 1A, composed of P. taichungensis and P. juntendi and applied underreduced fertilization, showed the lowest disease impact among infected treatments andsignificantly reduced severity relative to fertilized controls. PR genes were induced primarily by P.palmivora infection; transcriptional signals were highest in infected plants without the consortiumand lower in 1A-treated plants, indicating that phenotypic protection cannot be attributed solely tostronger induction of the four PR markers. Together, the results support a multimechanisticbiocontrol model in which bacterial functional complementarity is associated with directantagonism and reduced disease severity, while the contribution of host-mediated induced defenserequires broader molecular validation.
